            <ArticleTitle>Repetitive bleeding from a pheochromocytoma presenting as an abdominal emergency. Case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>69-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recurrent abdominal pain was the only subjective manifestation in a case of pheochromocytoma with retroperitoneal bleeding. At emergency laparotomy the tumor, showing signs of fresh and earlier bleeding, was extirpated. Sinus-type tachycardia was treated with beta-blockade peroperatively, while the diagnosis was still obscure, but hypertension did not follow. Meta-oxedrine and dopamine infusion was continued for 48 hours to sustain the blood pressure. Recovery was uneventful.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
